AB  - The tea geometrid (Ectropis grisescens) is a significant pest in Chinese tea plantations. Although Metarhizium anisopliae serves as an environmentally friendly biocontrol agent against E. grisescens, the molecular mechanisms underlying the insect's immune response remain unclear. This study investigates changes in immunity-related genes and metabolites in E. grisescens larvae infected with a virulent strain of M. anisopliae through transcriptome sequencing and metabolome analysis. We identified 2409 differentially expressed genes (DEGs) at 48 h post-infection, with 1611 genes up-regulated. GO analysis revealed that 119 DEGs were significantly enriched in immune-related processes. Additionally, 1860 differentially accumulated metabolites (DAMs) were detected, including 652 up-regulated and 1208 down-regulated metabolites, with 236 significantly enriched in 82 KEGG pathways. These findings indicate the activation of immunity-related and detoxifying enzyme-related genes, providing new insights into the physiological and biochemical responses of insects to biopesticides and potential targets for controlling tea geometrid.
